    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, a goblin? That's a weird way to put it lol. Anyway, back to the technical stuff—you mentioned the pressure spike. What happens if that tube gets blocked? Like, if something gets stuck inside the port, does that mess up the reading?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ha, fair point—goblins do have a way of sneaking into technical explanations! But yes, a blocked pitot tube is a classic goblin trap. If the ram air port gets clogged (say, by ice or a stubborn little critter), the dynamic pressure stays trapped inside, so the airspeed indicator will act like a barometer—it'll read whatever pressure was captured at the moment of blockage, often freezing or drifting with altitude changes. Meanwhile, if the static port is blocked, the diaphragm can't equalize, so the needle will read incorrectly—usually overreading on climb and underreading on descent. That's why pilots have a static port alternate source and a pitot heat switch—to keep those goblins out of the plumbing!",
      "source": "target",
